// Adaptation of the opening of Act I of The Importance of Being Earnest by
// Oscar Wilde, which premiered in 1895 and was published in book form in 1899.
// The play is in the United States public domain. This branching scene is an
// original adaptation: connective prose, choices, and story logic are newly
// written, and only short phrases are echoed from the source.

scene morning_room {
    background: "art/half_moon_street_morning_room.png"
}

stage algernon {
    image: "art/algernon_morning.png"
    name: "Algernon"
}

stage lane {
    image: "art/lane_serving.png"
    name: "Lane"
}

VAR sandwiches = 0
VAR questions = 0
FLAG heard_marriage = false

=== morning_room ===
@scene morning_room
A piano falls silent behind the folding doors of a bachelor's house in
Half-Moon Street, and the room keeps the hush that follows a performance.
@show algernon
@speaker algernon
"Did you hear what I was playing, Lane?"
@show lane
@speaker lane
"I didn't think it polite to listen, sir."
@speaker algernon
"Anyone can play accurately. I play with wonderful expression."
The remark is offered to the ceiling, which receives it without comment.
* [Ask after the cucumber sandwiches]
    SET sandwiches = sandwiches + 2
    "Have you got the cucumber sandwiches cut for Lady Bracknell?"
    The salver arrives, and two sandwiches leave it before it is set down.
    -> ledger
* [Ask about the champagne in the book]
    SET questions = questions + 1
    The account book is opened at the page for Thursday night.
    -> ledger
+ [Wave the subject away]
    Algernon turns to the window and lets the question go unanswered.
    -> ledger

=== ledger ===
The morning light lies across a small table set for tea, and the household
accounts wait beside it like a pupil called to recite.
{sandwiches > 0:
    The salver is lighter than it was. Algernon observes this without
    surprise, and helps himself to nothing further.
- else:
    The salver is still full. Algernon observes that too.
}
@speaker lane
"Eight bottles and a pint, sir."
@speaker algernon
"Why is it that at a bachelor's establishment the servants invariably drink
the champagne?"
@speaker lane
"I attribute it to the superior quality of the wine, sir."
@speaker algernon
The answer delights him more than the wine ever could.
* [Ask Lane whether marriage is demoralising]
    SET questions = questions + 1
    SET heard_marriage = true
    "Is marriage so demoralising as that?"
    Lane considers the question as if it were a rather good vintage.
    -> marriage
* [Let the subject rest]
    Algernon decides that the accounts have told him enough.
    -> marriage
+ [Return to the piano]
    The conversation is left standing where it was.
    -> marriage

=== marriage ===
{heard_marriage:
    Lane says he has only been married once, in consequence of a
    misunderstanding between himself and a young person.
- else:
    Lane offers no family history, and none is demanded of him.
}
@speaker lane
"It is not a very interesting subject. I never think of it myself."
@speaker algernon
"That will do, Lane, thank you."
@hide lane
@speaker
Lane withdraws, and the folding doors close behind him with a soft click.
* [Let him go]
    The bell for the next caller sounds somewhere below stairs.
    -> empty_house
* [Call him back for one last question]
    SET questions = questions + 1
    The doors part again, and Lane returns without any sign of impatience.
    -> last_word

=== empty_house ===
The morning-room is left to its owner, who plays a few bars with great
feeling and no accuracy at all. The sandwiches are gone, the accounts are
closed, and the household resumes its ordinary and entirely satisfactory
performance.
-> END

=== last_word ===
@show lane
@speaker lane
"Yes, sir?"
@speaker algernon
Algernon asks whether the lower orders might be persuaded to set a better
example, and Lane replies that he has never thought about it.
{questions > 2:
    The answer is so complete that Algernon cannot improve on it.
- else:
    The answer is brief, and Algernon lets it stand.
}
@hide lane
@speaker
The folding doors close. In the kitchen the champagne is put away, and
upstairs a piano begins again, a little less accurately than before.
-> END
